Names a happy feeling with no reason, or a reason with no feeling.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "Happy or excited, because the kite flew high and he laughed."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',3,'co21',2,'summary','{"type": "open", "passage": "Ben went to the beach with his gran. The sun was hot. They sat under a big red umbrella. Ben made a sandcastle with a tall tower. Then a wave came and washed it away. Ben felt sad. Gran said, \"Let us make a new one.\" This time they built it far from the sea.", "q": "Tell me in your own words what happened in the story.", "qno": 1, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Gives at least three events including the wave and the new castle.", "partial": "Gives one or two events.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "Ben and Gran went to the beach and built a sandcastle. A wave washed it away and Ben felt sad. They built a new one further from the sea."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',4,'co22',2,'inference','{"type": "open", "passage": "Ben went to the beach with his gran. The sun was hot. They sat under a big red umbrella. Ben made a sandcastle with a tall tower. Then a wave came and washed it away. Ben felt sad. Gran said, \"Let us make a new one.\" This time they built it far from the sea.", "q": "Why do you think Gran said, ''Let us make a new one''?", "qno": 2, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Links it to Ben feeling sad or wanting to cheer him up.", "partial": "Says only that the castle was gone or that they could play.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "To cheer Ben up because he was sad about the sandcastle."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',5,'co31',3,'summary','{"type": "open", "passage": "On Saturday, Priya''s class visited a farm. First, they fed the lambs with warm milk from big bottles. The lambs pushed and wriggled, and milk dripped down their chins. Then the farmer showed them how to collect eggs. Priya was nervous because the hens pecked at her fingers, but she found three brown eggs. At the end of the day the children were tired and muddy. On the bus, Priya fell asleep with an egg box on her knee.", "q": "Tell me about the day Priya''s class had.", "qno": 1, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Gives at least three parts of the day, including the ending.", "partial": "Gives one or two parts of the day.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "Priya''s class visited a farm, fed the lambs, and collected eggs. The children ended the day tired and muddy, and Priya fell asleep on the bus."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',6,'co32',3,'inference','{"type": "open", "passage": "On Saturday, Priya''s class visited a farm. First, they fed the lambs with warm milk from big bottles. The lambs pushed and wriggled, and milk dripped down their chins. Then the farmer showed them how to collect eggs. Priya was nervous because the hens pecked at her fingers, but she found three brown eggs. At the end of the day the children were tired and muddy. On the bus, Priya fell asleep with an egg box on her knee.", "q": "How can you tell that Priya had a busy day?", "qno": 2, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Gives a detail from the end of the passage AND links it to the day being busy or tiring.", "partial": "Gives a detail without the link, or a general statement without a detail.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "The children were tired and muddy, and Priya fell asleep on the bus."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',7,'co41',4,'summary','{"type": "open", "passage": "Mia found a small, wet kitten behind the garden shed. It was shivering and very thin. Mia wrapped the kitten in her scarf and carried it inside. Her dad warmed some milk in a saucer. Soon the kitten stopped shaking and began to purr. Mia asked if they could keep it, but Dad said they must first check whether it belonged to someone.", "q": "Tell me in your own words what happened in the story.", "qno": 1, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Gives at least three events including the kitten recovering and Dad''s condition.", "partial": "Gives one or two events.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "Mia found a cold, thin kitten behind the shed. She wrapped it in her scarf and took it inside. Dad warmed milk, the kitten began to purr, and Dad said they must check whether it belonged to someone."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',8,'co42',4,'inference','{"type": "open", "passage": "Mia found a small, wet kitten behind the garden shed. It was shivering and very thin. Mia wrapped the kitten in her scarf and carried it inside. Her dad warmed some milk in a saucer. Soon the kitten stopped shaking and began to purr. Mia asked if they could keep it, but Dad said they must first check whether it belonged to someone.", "q": "Why do you think Dad said they must check whether the kitten belonged to someone?", "qno": 2, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Says the kitten might have an owner who is missing it, or that it would be unfair to keep it.", "partial": "Says only that it might belong to someone (repeats the text).", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "Because it might have an owner who is looking for it and it would not be fair to keep someone else''s pet."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',9,'co51',5,'summary','{"type": "open", "passage": "Long ago, people crossing the desert used camels. A camel can drink a lot of water in a few minutes. Then it can walk for days without another drink. Its hump does not hold water, as many people think. It stores fat that the camel can use for energy. Thick eyelashes protect its eyes from sand, and wide feet stop it sinking. This is why camels were called ''ships of the desert''.", "q": "Explain in your own words why camels cope well in the desert.", "qno": 1, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Gives at least two features with what each does.", "partial": "Gives one feature.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "Camels can drink a lot and then go for days without water. The hump stores fat for energy. Thick eyelashes protect their eyes from sand and wide feet stop them sinking."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',10,'co52',5,'inference','{"type": "open", "passage": "Long ago, people crossing the desert used camels. A camel can drink a lot of water in a few minutes. Then it can walk for days without another drink. Its hump does not hold water, as many people think. It stores fat that the camel can use for energy. Thick eyelashes protect its eyes from sand, and wide feet stop it sinking. This is why camels were called ''ships of the desert''.", "q": "What do many people wrongly think about a camel''s hump?", "qno": 2, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "States the misconception (that it holds water).", "partial": "Mentions water or the hump but does not state the misconception clearly.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "They think it holds water, but it stores fat."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',11,'co61',6,'summary','{"type": "open", "passage": "The lighthouse on Gull Rock had stood for over two hundred years. Every night, its great lamp swept across the dark water, warning sailors to keep away from the jagged rocks below. When electric lights arrived, nobody needed the keeper any more. The lighthouse was locked up and left to the wind and the salt. Years later, the villagers decided to repair it. They painted its tower, mended the stairs and opened it as a museum. Now visitors climb to the top each summer to see the view the keeper once knew.", "q": "Tell the story of the lighthouse in your own words.", "qno": 1, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Gives at least three stages in order (job, abandoned, repaired or museum).", "partial": "Gives one or two stages.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "The lighthouse warned sailors with its lamp. When electric lights arrived the keeper was no longer needed and it was left empty. Years later the villagers repaired it and opened it as a museum."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',12,'co62',6,'inference','{"type": "open", "passage": "The lighthouse on Gull Rock had stood for over two hundred years. Every night, its great lamp swept across the dark water, warning sailors to keep away from the jagged rocks below. When electric lights arrived, nobody needed the keeper any more. The lighthouse was locked up and left to the wind and the salt. Years later, the villagers decided to repair it. They painted its tower, mended the stairs and opened it as a museum. Now visitors climb to the top each summer to see the view the keeper once knew.", "q": "Why do you think the villagers decided to repair the lighthouse?", "qno": 2, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Gives a reasoned motive (history, pride, visitors, keeping it for the future).", "partial": "Says only that it was old or broken.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "Because it was part of their history and they wanted to keep it and let visitors enjoy it."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',13,'co71',7,'summary','{"type": "open", "passage": "Scientists studying the Arctic have noticed that sea ice is forming later in autumn and melting earlier in spring than it did fifty years ago. For polar bears, which hunt seals from the ice, this shortens the season in which they can feed. Bears that cannot catch enough food in spring and summer must survive on their fat reserves until the ice returns. Some researchers argue that if the trend continues, fewer cubs will survive. Others point out that bears are adaptable and may find new food sources, although none of these provides as much energy as seal blubber. Both groups agree on one thing: more data are needed before firm predictions can be made.", "q": "Summarise the main point of the passage in two or three sentences.", "qno": 1, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Includes the change in ice, the effect on bears, and the disagreement or need for data.", "partial": "Includes only one of these ideas.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "Arctic sea ice forms later and melts earlier, so polar bears have less time to hunt seals. Researchers disagree about whether fewer cubs will survive or whether bears can adapt, and both agree more data are needed."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',14,'co72',7,'inference','{"type": "open", "passage": "Scientists studying the Arctic have noticed that sea ice is forming later in autumn and melting earlier in spring than it did fifty years ago. For polar bears, which hunt seals from the ice, this shortens the season in which they can feed. Bears that cannot catch enough food in spring and summer must survive on their fat reserves until the ice returns. Some researchers argue that if the trend continues, fewer cubs will survive. Others point out that bears are adaptable and may find new food sources, although none of these provides as much energy as seal blubber. Both groups agree on one thing: more data are needed before firm predictions can be made.", "q": "Explain the difference between the two groups of researchers'' views.", "qno": 2, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Describes both views accurately.", "partial": "Describes one view only.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "One group thinks fewer cubs will survive; the other thinks bears may adapt and find new food, although none gives as much energy as seal blubber."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',15,'co81',8,'summary','{"type": "open", "passage": "When the council announced plans to replace Millbrook Library with a car park, the response was swift. Within a week, a petition had gathered three thousand signatures. At the public meeting, a councillor insisted that ''the books are all online now anyway'', prompting a murmur of disbelief from the audience. A retired teacher then rose to describe how the library had taught her to read, and how, decades later, she volunteered there to teach others. Several students described the library as the only quiet place they could study. Though the council did not reverse its decision that evening, its chairman conceded that the strength of feeling had been ''greater than anticipated'', and promised to consider alternative sites. The campaigners left cautiously hopeful rather than triumphant.", "q": "Summarise what happened at the public meeting.", "qno": 1, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Gives at least three points, including the outcome.", "partial": "Gives one or two points.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "People objected to the plan to replace the library. A councillor said the books are online, a retired teacher and students spoke about the value of the library, and the council did not reverse its decision but promised to consider other sites."}'::jsonb),('comp_open',16,'co82',8,'inference','{"type": "open", "passage": "When the council announced plans to replace Millbrook Library with a car park, the response was swift. Within a week, a petition had gathered three thousand signatures. At the public meeting, a councillor insisted that ''the books are all online now anyway'', prompting a murmur of disbelief from the audience. A retired teacher then rose to describe how the library had taught her to read, and how, decades later, she volunteered there to teach others. Several students described the library as the only quiet place they could study. Though the council did not reverse its decision that evening, its chairman conceded that the strength of feeling had been ''greater than anticipated'', and promised to consider alternative sites. The campaigners left cautiously hopeful rather than triumphant.", "q": "Why might the retired teacher''s speech have been persuasive?", "qno": 2, "of": 2, "rubric": {"full": "Explains the effect of a personal, real-life example or its emotional impact.", "partial": "Says only that she liked the library or spoke well.", "zero": "Off topic, no response, or an answer that does not reflect the passage."}}'::jsonb,'{"accept": "She gave a personal example showing how the library helps people over many years, which makes the value real and emotional."}'::jsonb) on conflict (test_key,ord) do nothing;
